---
import Layout from '../../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import '../../../../styles/equipment.css';
const base='/equipment/07-13/', brochure='https://www.laboratory-equipment.com/pub/media/asset-library/l/s/ls_series_cryogenic_refrigerators_worthington_industries.pdf', installed='https://hylandscientific.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/11/VBS-2019-62.jpg', later='https://www.scilogex.com/web/image/product.template/4501/image_1920?unique=83a7c7e';
---
<Layout pageTitle="Worthington LS6000 · sealed exterior and detached artwork diagram" description="Inspect the closed Worthington LS6000 casing and compare unrelated geometric poster layouts." wide={true}>
<article class="equipment ls"><nav class="eq-nav"><a href="/simulations/equipment/07-13/">← Equipment 07-13</a><a href="/simulations/equipment/">Equipment register</a><a href="#ls-evidence">References and verification</a></nav>
<header class="eq-mast"><span class="eq-meta">07-13 · DOCUMENTARY EXTERIOR & DETACHED ARTWORK / FIRST PASS</span><h2>Worthington LS6000</h2><p>Orbit the sealed LS6000 exterior. On a physically separate teaching display, see why three rectangles can have the same total area but cover less area together when they overlap. The diagram is not a layout for the instrument.</p></header>
<div class="grid"><section><h3>Closed, standing vessel silhouette</h3><div id="ls-view" aria-label="Orbitable closed Worthington LS6000 outline and independent artwork display"></div>
<div class="bar"><button data-ls-view="overall">Reset view</button><button data-ls-view="shell">Cap and shoulders</button><button data-ls-view="display">Detached artwork</button><button id="ls-cycle">Next poster layout</button></div>
<p id="ls-ready" role="status">Loading metric model…</p><p>Drag to orbit and pinch or scroll to zoom. The teal badge changes only the detached poster. The cap, body and external handles are fixed and the interior is absent.</p></section>
<section><h3>Unrelated artwork rectangles</h3><canvas id="ls-plot" width="660" height="446" aria-label="Fixed graphic poster with three labeled rectangles; edge-touching, overlap, and outside examples"></canvas>
<div class="fields"><label>Fixed layout<select id="ls-case"><option value="separate">Separated poster tiles</option><option value="touching">Edges exactly touch</option><option value="overlap">One printed overlap</option><option value="outside">One tile partly outside</option></select></label></div>
<div class="bar"><button class="primary" id="ls-inspect">Inspect poster areas</button><button id="ls-reset">Reset</button></div><p id="ls-status" aria-live="polite">Choose a fixed layout and inspect its rectangles.</p><output id="ls-result"></output><div class="bar"><button id="ls-json" disabled>Inspection JSON</button></div></section></div>
<section id="ls-evidence"><h3>Geometry and evidence</h3><p>Selected variant: standalone non-AT Worthington LS6000, without a cart or external module. <a href={brochure+'#page=2'}>Worthington LS Series brochure, p. 2</a> provides the nominal 683 mm diameter and 991 mm height. A <a href={installed}>legacy Taylor-Wharton LS6000 exterior photo</a> shows the carried shoulder shape and ribs; a <a href={later}>later closed LS6000 variant product image</a> corroborates the barrel form without the extra cart. The register also names a CS100 reference configuration; the physically paired LS6000 installation was not verified, so no CS100 is shown. Local shapes and radii are visual estimates.</p>
<div class="grid"><figure><img width="660" height="440" src={base+'front.webp'} loading="lazy" alt="Original whole closed LS6000 rendering with a separate poster display"/><figcaption>Original exterior render; the later model photo corroborates silhouette, banding and blue cap. The teaching monitor is unrelated.</figcaption></figure><figure><img width="660" height="440" src={base+'detail.webp'} loading="lazy" alt="Original cap and shoulder comparison of fixed closed parts"/><figcaption>Original detail compared with the legacy exterior photo. The cap and handles are stationary abstractions, not working parts.</figcaption></figure></div>
<div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Independent reference</th><th>Actual / interpretation</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Nominal closed silhouette</td><td>683 mm diameter × 991 mm height</td><td>Verified in metric GLB along both transverse axes and in height.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Three detached rectangles</td><td>600 + 500 + 500 mm²</td><td>1,600 mm² distinct area both when separated and touching an edge.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Overlapping poster</td><td>8 × 15 mm common area</td><td>Subtract 120 mm² counted twice: 1,480 mm² total union.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Boundary spot</td><td>Tile B reaches X = 107 mm, board edge X = 100 mm</td><td>−7 mm independent signed poster clearance. Union includes all drawn footprints, even outside the board.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Original equipment acceptance</td><td>Physical storage and a recorded sample history</td><td>Not implemented or run. No contents, storage events, controller, cooling, conditions, handling, or preparation.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>The unrelated art poster divides the X-axis at rectangle edges, merges overlapping Y intervals inside each strip, and multiplies merged width and height for total covered area. A shared boundary alone has zero area. A separate signed board-edge gap flags any part drawn outside. JSON preserves immutable artwork IDs, positions, signed gaps, units and provenance. There is no apparatus action, physical access or real-world compatibility claim.</p>
<p><a href={base+'worthington-ls6000-static.glb'} download>Metric GLB</a> · <a href={base+'model.py'} download>Editable Blender generator</a> · <a href={base+'layout-example.json'}>Output schema example</a> · <a href={base+'verification.json'}>Verification</a> · <a href={base+'xr-manifest.json'}>VR action contract</a>. No headset or live hardware was tested.</p></section>
</article></Layout>
<script>import '../../../../lib/equipment/07-13/browser.mjs';</script>
<style>
.ls{max-width:76rem}.ls .grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr));gap:1.1rem}.ls section{border-top:1px solid var(--border);padding:.8rem 0;margin:.8rem 0;min-width:0}.ls .bar{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:.44rem;margin:.55rem 0}.ls .fields{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr));gap:.48rem}.ls label{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:.2rem;font-size:.92rem}.ls input,.ls select,.ls button{font:inherit;color:var(--text);background:var(--panel);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:.14rem;max-width:100%;min-width:0;min-height:2.4em;padding:.32em .52em}.ls button{cursor:pointer}.ls button:disabled{opacity:.52;cursor:default}.ls .primary{color:white;background:#245350}.ls #ls-view{height:clamp(270px,31vw,435px);background:#d9dddf;touch-action:none}.ls #ls-view :global(canvas){width:100%;height:100%;display:block}.ls #ls-plot,.ls img{width:100%;height:auto}.ls #ls-result{display:block;white-space:pre-line;margin:.45rem 0;font:1rem monospace}.ls #ls-status.error{color:#a54a34}.ls .scroll{overflow:auto}.ls table{border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.89rem}.ls td,.ls th{border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);text-align:left;padding:.45rem}.ls figure{margin:.8rem 0}@media(max-width:800px){.ls .grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}}
</style>
